        <div class="content">
            <h2>What Is a Timed Agenda?</h2>
            <p>A timed agenda is a meeting agenda where every topic gets a specific time slot — a start time and a duration — instead of just a bullet point. "Project update" becomes "2:05–2:20 Project update (15 min)". That one change forces the two decisions that make meetings work: what actually matters, and how much of everyone's time it deserves.</p>
            <p>Without time allocations, the first topic eats 40 of your 60 minutes and the decision you actually scheduled the meeting for gets 5 rushed minutes at the end. With them, overruns are visible per topic, while there's still time to correct course.</p>

            <h2>Timed Agenda Template: 30-Minute Team Meeting</h2>
            <p>Copy this structure and swap in your topics. The times matter more than the topics — note that the biggest block goes to the decision, not the updates.</p>
            <table class="agenda-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Time slot</th><th>Duration</th><th>Topic</th><th>Output</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:00 – 0:03</td><td>3 min</td><td>Check-in and agenda review</td><td>Everyone knows the plan</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:03 – 0:10</td><td>7 min</td><td>Status updates (blockers only)</td><td>Blockers surfaced</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:10 – 0:22</td><td>12 min</td><td>Main decision or discussion</td><td>A decision, made</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:22 – 0:27</td><td>5 min</td><td>Open floor / parking lot items</td><td>Nothing left simmering</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:27 – 0:30</td><td>3 min</td><td>Action items and owners</td><td>Who does what, by when</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h2>Timed Agenda Template: 60-Minute Planning Meeting</h2>
            <table class="agenda-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Time slot</th><th>Duration</th><th>Topic</th><th>Output</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:00 – 0:05</td><td>5 min</td><td>Goals for this session</td><td>Shared definition of done</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:05 – 0:15</td><td>10 min</td><td>Review: what happened last cycle</td><td>Context, not blame</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:15 – 0:35</td><td>20 min</td><td>Prioritize the backlog</td><td>Ranked list</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:35 – 0:50</td><td>15 min</td><td>Assign work and estimate</td><td>Owners and estimates</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:50 – 0:57</td><td>7 min</td><td>Risks and dependencies</td><td>Known unknowns listed</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="time-col">0:57 – 1:00</td><td>3 min</td><td>Recap and action items</td><td>Written commitments</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h2>How to Allocate Time to Agenda Items</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Work backward from the end time.</strong> The meeting ends when it ends. Fit topics into that, not the other way around.</li>
                <li><strong>Budget by output, not by topic size.</strong> A decision needs more time than a status update, even if the update has more slides.</li>
                <li><strong>Put the most important topic early.</strong> Energy and attention are front-loaded. Don't spend them on housekeeping.</li>
                <li><strong>Reserve the last 3–5 minutes for action items.</strong> A meeting without written follow-ups is a conversation, not a meeting.</li>
                <li><strong>Cut anything that's pure broadcast.</strong> If a topic is one person talking and nobody deciding, it's an email.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="quote-block">
                Parkinson's Law: work expands to fill the time available for its completion. A timed agenda is how you shrink the container. See the data on <a href="/meeting-length-effectiveness" style="color: #1a73e8;">how long meetings should actually be</a>.
            </div>

            <h2>A Timed Agenda Only Works If Everyone Can See It</h2>
            <p>Here's where most timed agendas die: they live in a calendar invite nobody reopens once the meeting starts. The facilitator becomes the human timer, awkwardly interrupting people — which is exactly the job nobody wants.</p>
            <p>The fix is making the clock visible to the whole room. AgendaClock turns your timed agenda into a live visual timeline with a <a href="/meeting-agenda-timer" style="color: #1a73e8;">countdown timer for each topic</a>. Share it in your video call, or <a href="/obs-setup" style="color: #1a73e8;">overlay it on your camera feed with OBS</a> so the agenda rides along in your video. When everyone can see the time remaining, people self-regulate — you never have to be the one who cuts someone off. That also helps with <a href="/managing-talkative-participants" style="color: #1a73e8;">participants who talk too much</a>.</p>

            <div class="faq-section">
                <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <h3>What is a timed agenda?</h3>
                    <p>A meeting agenda where every topic has a start time and duration instead of just a bullet point. The time allocations force prioritization before the meeting and keep discussion on track during it.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <h3>How do I add time slots to a meeting agenda?</h3>
                    <p>Work backward from the end time. List the topics, decide what each one needs to produce (a decision, an update, a plan), then assign minutes based on that output. Decisions get more time than status updates, the most important topic goes first, and the last few minutes are reserved for action items.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <h3>What's the difference between a timed agenda and a regular agenda?</h3>
                    <p>A regular agenda lists what will be discussed. A timed agenda also says for how long. Topics get prioritized in advance, discussions self-regulate when time remaining is visible, and overruns get caught at the topic level instead of at the end of the meeting.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <h3>How strictly should I enforce the time slots?</h3>
                    <p>Treat the timer as a checkpoint, not a guillotine. When a topic hits its limit, decide explicitly: extend it by borrowing from a lower-priority item, or park it for follow-up. Never drift past the limit silently — that trains everyone to ignore the agenda.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

